== Motivation ==

BIP 445 specifies FROST signing with BIP 340-compatible signatures but leaves key generation to a separate protocol. RFC 9591 provides a trusted-dealer construction, but implementations also need agreement on Bitcoin-specific key representations, participant identifiers, validation, and the relationship between key generation and Taproot tweaking.

This proposal defines those rules for deployments that accept a trusted setup party, such as a provisioning service or trusted hardware device. Feldman commitments let participants check their shares against a common polynomial and derive the public signing material independently. Deployments in which no single party may learn the complete secret require a distributed key-generation protocol, such as [https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/bip-frost-dkg ChillDKG].

== Specification ==

The key words MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, and MAY specify implementation requirements. The algorithms below define the computational behavior; the distribution and completion requirements also apply to a successful key-generation ceremony. An algorithm that fails MUST NOT return usable or partial key material.

=== Overview and Scope ===

The '''dealer''' constructs a sharing polynomial, computes its Feldman commitments, applies the key-generation transformation, and distributes the final shares. Each '''participant''' verifies its share and establishes that all participants accepted the same final commitment. A participant selected for a later signing session is a '''signer'''. The dealer need not be a participant or take part in signing.

The procedure consists of:

# [[#Trusted Dealer Key Generation|Generating]] the final shares and commitments.
# [[#Secret-Share Distribution|Distributing]] each secret share with its authenticated context.
# [[#Participant Key Acceptance|Verifying]] the participant's share and public data.
# [[#Completion and Failure Handling|Completing]] the ceremony and deleting dealer-held secrets.

This proposal does not define signing, nonce generation, share refresh or repair, participant replacement, secret recovery, transport wire formats, wallet backups, or Bitcoin transaction and Taproot script construction. Requirements on transport and secret handling still apply even though their implementation is application-specific.

=== Parameters and Outputs ===

Key generation takes a participant count ''n'', a threshold ''t'', and optionally an initial secret ''seckey''. The parameters MUST satisfy ''1 ≤ t ≤ n ≤ 128'', matching [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445].

Participant identifiers are the integers ''id = 0, ..., n - 1''. Participant ''id'' has the nonzero Shamir evaluation point ''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''. Implementations MUST NOT use ''id'' itself as the evaluation point or renumber participants when selecting a signing set. When serialized, ''id'' uses exactly four unsigned big-endian bytes, as in BIP 445.

Successful key generation produces:

{| class="wikitable"
! Value !! Representation !! Visibility
|-
| ''n'', ''t'' || Participant count and threshold || Public
|-
| ''thresh_pk'' || 33-byte compressed threshold public key || Public
|-
| ''pubshares'' || ''n'' compressed public shares in identifier order || Public
|-
| ''vss_commitment'' || ''33t'' bytes, in coefficient order || Public
|-
| ''secshares[id]'' || 32-byte nonzero scalar for participant ''id'' || Private to that participant
|}

The dealer's output contains all shares for distribution; each participant receives only its own. Public data requires integrity and association with the ceremony, but not confidentiality. The VSS commitment is used for key-generation verification and is not an input to signing.

=== Notation ===

This specification uses the secp256k1 group, scalar arithmetic, and serialization functions defined in [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing#notation BIP 445's Notation section]. In particular, ''G'', ''inf_point'', ''cbytes'', ''cpoint'', ''cbytes_ext'', ''cpoint_ext'', ''xbytes'', ''scalar_to_bytes'', ''scalar_from_bytes_checked'', and ''scalar_from_bytes_nonzero_checked'' have the meanings specified there. These definitions, including their encoding lengths and validation rules, are normative here.

We write ''q'' for the group order called ''ord'' in BIP 445. All scalar arithmetic is modulo ''q''; inversion requires a nonzero scalar. ''Scalar(x)'' converts an integer satisfying ''0 ≤ x < q'' to a scalar. Lists in this document are zero-indexed, slices exclude the upper bound, ''||'' concatenates byte strings, and ''None'' denotes an omitted optional input.

Write the initial polynomial and Feldman commitments as:

:''f(x) = Σ<sub>j=0</sub><sup>t-1</sup> a<sub>j</sub>x<sup>j</sup>'',
:''A<sub>j</sub> = a<sub>j</sub>G''.

Use ''b<sub>j</sub>'', ''B<sub>j</sub>'', and ''f<sub>final</sub>'' for the corresponding values after the mandatory key-generation transformation. Non-constant coefficients MAY be zero, including the highest coefficient, so the polynomial has degree at most ''t - 1''.

Parsers MUST reject incorrect lengths and noncanonical encodings. Imported secrets and final shares MUST use the nonzero scalar parser and MUST NOT be repaired by reduction, hashing, truncation, clamping, or another transformation. Explicit transformations of already valid scalars are separate from parsing.

''thresh_pk'' and participant public shares MUST use ordinary compressed encodings of non-infinity points. They need not have even Y; signing-time parity adjustment follows BIP 445.

''vss_commitment'' uses the layout specified in [[#Parsing a VSS Commitment|Parsing a VSS Commitment]].

=== Randomness and Secret Import ===

The dealer MUST use cryptographically secure randomness. A generated initial secret is sampled uniformly from ''{1, ..., q - 1}''. Each non-constant coefficient is sampled independently and uniformly from ''{0, ..., q - 1}''.

Random scalar generation SHOULD follow [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591#appendix-D RFC 9591 Appendix D]. Rejection sampling of 32 random bytes is one suitable method. Simply reducing a random 32-byte integer modulo ''q'' MUST NOT be used as a uniform sampler. Zero candidates for a nonzero secret are discarded and sampled again.

An imported ''seckey'' MUST be a canonical nonzero scalar derived from at least 32 bytes of entropy, as required by RFC 9591 Appendix C for this scalar size. Its supplier is responsible for its entropy and confidentiality; these cannot be established from the encoding. Importing a secret does not repair weak generation or prior disclosure.

The imported scalar is the '''initial''' constant coefficient. The mandatory transformation changes the final shared secret, so importing ''seckey'' does not generally preserve the public key ''seckey * G''.

=== Polynomial Evaluation ===

''PolynomialEvaluate'' and ''PolynomialEvaluatePoint'' use Horner's method. Input lists are stored in ascending coefficient order, with the constant coefficient first, and MUST be non-empty. Evaluation processes them in reverse, from the highest-degree coefficient to the constant coefficient. For example, ''[a<sub>0</sub>, a<sub>1</sub>, a<sub>2</sub>]'' evaluates to ''(a<sub>2</sub>x + a<sub>1</sub>)x + a<sub>0</sub>''. This avoids computing powers of ''x'' explicitly. The same recurrence applies to group commitments, which MAY include infinity elements.

Algorithm ''PolynomialEvaluate(coeffs, x)'':
* Inputs:
** The coefficient list ''coeffs'': scalars in ascending coefficient order
** The evaluation point ''x'': a scalar
* Fail if ''len(coeffs) == 0''.
* Let ''value = Scalar(0)''.
* For each ''coeff'' in ''coeffs'', in reverse order:
** Let ''value = value · x + coeff''.
* Return ''value''.

Algorithm ''PolynomialEvaluatePoint(commitments, x)'':
* Inputs:
** The commitment list ''commitments'': group elements in ascending coefficient order
** The evaluation point ''x'': a scalar
* Fail if ''len(commitments) == 0''.
* Let ''result = inf_point''.
* For each ''A'' in ''commitments'', in reverse order:
** Let ''result = x · result + A''.
* Return ''result''.

Algorithm ''ParticipantEvaluationPoint(id, n)'':
* Inputs:
** The participant identifier ''id'': an integer
** The participant count ''n'': an integer
* Fail unless ''1 ≤ n ≤ 128''.
* Fail unless ''0 ≤ id < n''.
* Return ''Scalar(id + 1)''.

=== Parsing a VSS Commitment ===

The final commitment is serialized in coefficient order as:

:''vss_commitment = cbytes_ext(B<sub>0</sub>) || ... || cbytes_ext(B<sub>t-1</sub>)''.

It MUST contain exactly ''33t'' bytes. ''B<sub>0</sub>'' MUST NOT be infinity; non-constant commitments MAY be infinity, encoded as 33 zero bytes by ''cbytes_ext''. Commitment elements MUST NOT be reordered, omitted, or duplicated. The extended encoding does not permit infinity in an ordinary public key.

Algorithm ''ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)'':
* Inputs:
** The serialized commitment ''vss_commitment'': a byte array containing the coefficient commitments in order
** The threshold ''t'': an integer
* Fail if ''t < 1''.
* Fail unless ''len(vss_commitment) == 33 · t''.
* Let ''commitments'' be an empty list.
* For ''j = 0, ..., t - 1'':
** Let ''B = cpoint_ext(vss_commitment[33 · j : 33 · (j + 1)])''; fail if parsing fails.
** Fail if ''j == 0'' and ''B == inf_point''.
** Append ''B'' to ''commitments''.
* Return ''commitments''.

=== Taproot-Safety Transformation ===

The dealer MUST apply the transformation below before distributing shares. Define the BIP 341 tagged hash by:

:''hash<sub>TapTweak</sub>(m) = SHA256(SHA256("TapTweak") || SHA256("TapTweak") || m)'',

where the tag is encoded as ASCII. The hash input is the initial point's X coordinate without a Merkle root. Its integer value MUST be less than ''q''; it is not reduced modulo ''q''.

Algorithm ''TaprootSafetyTweak(P)'':
* Input:
** The initial constant commitment ''P'': a group element
* Fail if ''P == inf_point''.
* Let ''h = hash<sub>TapTweak</sub>(xbytes(P))''.
* Let ''v'' be the unsigned big-endian integer represented by ''h''.
* Fail if ''v ≥ q''.
* Return ''Scalar(v)''.

Algorithm ''ApplyTaprootSafety(coeffs, commitments)'':
* Inputs:
** The initial coefficient list ''coeffs'': scalars in ascending coefficient order
** The initial commitment list ''commitments'': group elements satisfying ''commitments[j] = coeffs[j] · G'' at each index ''j''
* Fail unless ''len(coeffs) == len(commitments)'' and ''len(commitments) ≥ 1''.
* Fail if ''commitments[0] == inf_point''.
* Let ''tau = TaprootSafetyTweak(commitments[0])''; fail if that fails.
* Let ''final_coeffs'' be a copy of ''coeffs'' and ''final_commitments'' be a copy of ''commitments''.
* Let ''final_coeffs[0] = coeffs[0] + tau''.
* Let ''final_commitments[0] = commitments[0] + tau · G''.
* Fail if ''final_coeffs[0] == Scalar(0)''.
* Fail if ''final_commitments[0] == inf_point''.
* Fail unless ''final_commitments[0] == final_coeffs[0] · G''.
* Return ''(final_coeffs, final_commitments, tau)''.

Thus ''f<sub>final</sub>(x) = f(x) + tau'', ''B<sub>0</sub> = A<sub>0</sub> + tau G'', and ''B<sub>j</sub> = A<sub>j</sub>'' for ''j > 0''. Every share is shifted by the same scalar. This transformation uses the initial full point without first normalizing it to even Y.

=== Public-Key Derivation and Share Verification ===

The final VSS commitment is authoritative. The threshold point is ''B<sub>0</sub>''; participant ''id'' has public point ''P<sub>id</sub> = Σ<sub>j=0</sub><sup>t-1</sup> (id + 1)<sup>j</sup>B<sub>j</sub>''. A separately supplied public key MUST match the value derived from this commitment.

Algorithm ''DerivePubshareFromCommitment(id, n, t, vss_commitment)'':
* Inputs:
** The participant identifier ''id'': an integer
** The participant count ''n'' and threshold ''t'': integers
** The final serialized commitment ''vss_commitment'': a byte array
* Fail unless ''1 ≤ t ≤ n ≤ 128''.
* Let ''x = ParticipantEvaluationPoint(id, n)''; fail if that fails.
* Let ''commitments = ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)''; fail if that fails.
* Let ''P = PolynomialEvaluatePoint(commitments, x)''.
* Fail if ''P == inf_point''.
* Return ''cbytes(P)''.

Algorithm ''ThreshPubkeyFromCommitment(t, vss_commitment)'':
* Inputs:
** The threshold ''t'': an integer
** The final serialized commitment ''vss_commitment'': a byte array
* Let ''commitments = ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)''; fail if parsing fails.
* Return ''cbytes(commitments[0])''.

Algorithm ''VerifySecshare(id, n, t, secshare, vss_commitment)'':
* Inputs:
** The participant identifier ''id'': an integer
** The participant count ''n'' and threshold ''t'': integers
** The final secret share ''secshare'': a 32-byte array encoding a nonzero scalar
** The final serialized commitment ''vss_commitment'': a byte array
* Return ''False'' unless ''1 ≤ t ≤ n ≤ 128''.
* Let ''d = scalar_from_bytes_nonzero_checked(secshare)''; return ''False'' if parsing fails.
* Let ''x = ParticipantEvaluationPoint(id, n)''; return ''False'' if that fails.
* Let ''commitments = ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)''; return ''False'' if that fails.
* Return ''True'' if ''d · G == PolynomialEvaluatePoint(commitments, x)'', otherwise return ''False''.

Verification compares group elements. Since ''d'' is nonzero, an infinity expected point fails verification without needing to serialize it. Public-share derivation and secret-share verification validate ''n'' and ''t'', but these helpers do not establish transport authentication or commitment agreement; participants MUST perform the complete acceptance procedure below.

=== Trusted Dealer Key Generation ===

''TrustedDealerKeygen(n, t, seckey)'' generates an initial coefficient list and invokes the deterministic algorithm below.

Algorithm ''TrustedDealerKeygen(n, t, seckey)'':
* Inputs:
** The participant count ''n'' and threshold ''t'': integers
** The initial secret ''seckey'': a 32-byte array encoding a nonzero scalar (optional; ''None'' if omitted)
* Fail unless ''1 ≤ t ≤ n ≤ 128''.
* If ''seckey'' is supplied:
** Let ''a<sub>0</sub> = scalar_from_bytes_nonzero_checked(seckey)''; fail if parsing fails.
* Else:
** Let ''a<sub>0</sub>'' be a uniformly sampled nonzero scalar.
* Let ''coeffs = [a<sub>0</sub>]''.
* For ''j = 1, ..., t - 1'':
** Append an independently uniformly sampled scalar to ''coeffs''.
* Return ''TrustedDealerKeygenInternal(coeffs, n)''.

If the internal invocation fails because a '''final''' participant share is zero and ''t > 1'', the dealer MAY resample the entire set of non-constant coefficients and retry while retaining ''a0''. No individual share may be repaired. Any other failure, including a Taproot-safety failure, causes the current operation to fail whether the initial secret was generated or imported. An imported secret MUST NOT be modified to repair failure.

=== Internal Trusted Dealer Key Generation ===

''TrustedDealerKeygenInternal(coeffs, n)'' takes a non-empty list of canonical scalars in ascending coefficient order and produces the final serialized material. It MUST NOT sample randomness. An implementation SHOULD expose an equivalent deterministic operation for testing; production generation MUST NOT substitute fixed test coefficients for secure randomness.

Internal Algorithm ''TrustedDealerKeygenInternal(coeffs, n)'':
* Inputs:
** The initial coefficient list ''coeffs'': canonical scalars in ascending coefficient order
** The participant count ''n'': an integer
* Let ''t = len(coeffs)''.
* Fail unless ''1 ≤ t ≤ n ≤ 128''.
* Fail if ''coeffs[0] == Scalar(0)''.
* Let ''commitments'' be the list of points ''coeff · G'' for each ''coeff'' in ''coeffs'', in order.
* Fail if ''commitments[0] == inf_point''.
* Let ''(final_coeffs, final_commitments, tau) = ApplyTaprootSafety(coeffs, commitments)''; fail if that fails.
* Let ''vss_commitment'' be the concatenation of ''cbytes_ext(B)'' for each ''B'' in ''final_commitments'', in order.
* Let ''thresh_pk = cbytes(final_commitments[0])''.
* Let ''secshares'' and ''pubshares'' be empty lists.
* For ''id = 0, ..., n - 1'':
** Let ''x = ParticipantEvaluationPoint(id, n)''.
** Let ''d = PolynomialEvaluate(final_coeffs, x)''.
** Fail if ''d == Scalar(0)''.
** Let ''P = d · G''.
** Fail if ''P == inf_point''.
** Fail unless ''P == PolynomialEvaluatePoint(final_commitments, x)''.
** Append ''scalar_to_bytes(d)'' to ''secshares''.
** Append ''cbytes(P)'' to ''pubshares''.
* Fail unless ''ThreshPubkeyFromCommitment(t, vss_commitment) == thresh_pk''.
* For ''id = 0, ..., n - 1'':
** Fail unless ''VerifySecshare(id, n, t, secshares[id], vss_commitment)'' returns ''True''.
* Return ''(vss_commitment, thresh_pk, pubshares, secshares)''.

All called operations MUST succeed. The lists are ordered by participant identifier. Only final shares are required to be nonzero: a zero evaluation of the initial polynomial does not itself cause failure. No initial, intermediate, or rejected sharing may be distributed as output.

=== Secret-Share Distribution ===

After dealer-side validation, the dealer MUST deliver ''secshares[id]'' only to its intended participant over a mutually authenticated channel providing confidentiality and integrity. The share MUST be authenticated together with at least ''(n, t, id, vss_commitment)''. Retransmitting the same share to its intended participant is permitted.

The application defines the transport and serialization of this context. It MAY additionally bind a ceremony identifier, transcript identifier, device identity, or network identity. A share from another ceremony MUST NOT be accepted merely because it uses the same participant identifier.

All participants MUST establish a consistent view of the same final ''vss_commitment''. Its integrity and association with the delivered share MUST be protected. Authenticated point-to-point delivery alone does not establish agreement; an application MAY use a secure authenticated broadcast mechanism. A participant unable to establish the required association or agreement MUST reject the result.

=== Participant Key Acceptance ===

Each participant MUST perform the checks below and the distribution checks above before using its share. ''thresh_pk'' and ''pubshares'' MAY be omitted when derived exclusively from the commitment.

Algorithm ''ParticipantKeyAccept(id, n, t, secshare, vss_commitment, thresh_pk, pubshares)'':
* Inputs:
** The participant identifier ''id'': an integer
** The participant count ''n'' and threshold ''t'': integers
** The final secret share ''secshare'': a 32-byte array encoding a nonzero scalar
** The final serialized commitment ''vss_commitment'': a byte array
** The supplied threshold public key ''thresh_pk'': a 33-byte compressed public key (optional; ''None'' if omitted)
** The supplied public shares ''pubshares'': a list of ''n'' 33-byte compressed public keys in participant identifier order (optional; ''None'' if omitted)
* Fail unless ''1 ≤ t ≤ n ≤ 128''.
* Fail unless ''0 ≤ id < n''.
* Let ''commitments = ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)''; fail if parsing fails.
* Fail unless ''VerifySecshare(id, n, t, secshare, vss_commitment)'' returns ''True''.
* Let ''derived_thresh_pk = ThreshPubkeyFromCommitment(t, vss_commitment)''; fail if that fails.
* Let ''derived_pubshare = DerivePubshareFromCommitment(id, n, t, vss_commitment)''; fail if that fails.
* If ''thresh_pk'' is supplied:
** Parse ''cpoint(thresh_pk)''; fail if parsing fails.
** Fail unless ''thresh_pk == derived_thresh_pk''.
* If ''pubshares'' is supplied:
** Fail unless ''len(pubshares) == n''.
** For ''j = 0, ..., n - 1'':
*** Let ''derived = DerivePubshareFromCommitment(j, n, t, vss_commitment)''; fail if that fails.
*** Parse ''cpoint(pubshares[j])''; fail if parsing fails.
*** Fail unless ''pubshares[j] == derived''.
* Establish authenticated delivery, ceremony binding, and agreement on the final commitment through the application; fail unless the [[#Secret-Share Distribution|distribution requirements]] are satisfied.
* Return the private record ''(id, secshare)'' and the public record ''(n, t, derived_thresh_pk, pubshares, vss_commitment)''.

If ''pubshares'' was omitted, the accepted public record may omit it; the complete list MAY subsequently be derived from the commitment using ''DerivePubshareFromCommitment'' for each identifier. Any supplied individual public share MUST likewise match its derived value. A failed check MUST NOT be repaired by changing a share, identifier, evaluation point, commitment, or supplied public key.

=== Completion and Failure Handling ===

A ceremony completes only after dealer-side checks succeed, final shares are delivered, and every designated participant accepts its share under the same final commitment. If any participant rejects or fails to establish agreement, the ceremony MUST NOT be declared successful.

An application MAY start a new attempt. It MUST NOT silently remove or replace a participant, change ''n'' or ''t'', combine material from different attempts, or use partial output to complete the original ceremony. Failed and superseded material MUST NOT be used for signing.

After successful completion the dealer MUST delete secret state that is no longer required: initial and final secrets, coefficients, copies of participant shares, secret sampling material, and temporary or serialized copies. Secret state MUST remain confidential while it exists and MUST NOT be logged, persisted, exposed through diagnostics, or sent to others except as required for share delivery. Subsequent signing MUST NOT depend on retained dealer secrets.

Logical deletion means the implementation no longer intentionally retains or uses these values. Implementations SHOULD minimize secret lifetimes and copies and use secure-memory and zeroization facilities where available. Public state MAY be retained with its integrity and ceremony association protected.

=== Use with BIP 445 ===

A signing session selects ''u'' distinct participants, with ''1 ≤ t ≤ u ≤ n ≤ 128'', and uses their original identifiers, accepted secret shares, corresponding public shares, and common threshold public key. Long-term material MUST NOT be altered except by this specification's transformation or applicable BIP 445 algorithms. Signing-context validation does not replace participant acceptance.

[https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445] represents public key material as ''Threshold Info'' and uses a ''Session Context'' for signing. The participant limit in this proposal follows its security requirement of ''n ≤ 128''.

The output ''thresh_pk'' is a 33-byte plain public key. The word ''plain'' describes its representation, not whether it has been transformed. It is not a serialized Taproot witness program.

Applications MAY use the final key as a Taproot internal key and apply further tweaks through BIP 445 and [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0341.mediawiki BIP 341]. These signing-time tweaks do not modify the long-term shares. X-only interpretation and parity handling follow BIP 340, BIP 341, and BIP 445. A script-tree tweak MUST include the applicable Merkle root; an output without a spendable script path follows BIP 341's no-script-path construction.

== Rationale ==

=== Trusted Dealer and Verifiable Sharing ===

A trusted dealer provides a simple setup when a deployment already accepts a party with access to the complete secret. Distributed key generation is the alternative when that trust is unacceptable. Feldman commitments allow each participant to check its share and independently derive the public material, but do not prove honest randomness or erasure. A proof of knowledge used in a DKG protocol would not remove the dealer's knowledge of the secret in this trust model.

=== Identifiers and Encodings ===

The ''id + 1'' mapping combines zero-based protocol identifiers with the distinct, nonzero evaluation points required by Shamir sharing. Using zero itself would disclose the constant coefficient to participant zero. Compressed points preserve the parity information needed by BIP 445; extended encoding also represents the zero coefficients permitted by RFC 9591. Canonical parsing prevents different encodings from representing the same accepted value.

=== Preservation of Share Consistency ===

Adding ''tau'' to the polynomial's constant coefficient adds ''tau'' to every secret share and ''tau G'' to the corresponding public points, preserving Feldman verification. The final polynomial still has degree at most ''t - 1'', so any ''t'' distinct participants' shares reconstruct its constant coefficient, and their public shares reconstruct the threshold public key. The underlying Shamir and Feldman constructions are described in [https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9591.html#appendix-C RFC 9591 Appendix C].

== Security Considerations ==

=== Dealer Trust and Randomness ===

The dealer knows the complete secret and all shares. A malicious or compromised dealer can compromise the key regardless of ''t''. Deployments that prohibit any single party from learning the secret MUST use distributed key generation instead.

Share verification establishes consistency with the accepted commitment. It cannot establish unbiased randomness, confidentiality, exclusive delivery, or erasure. Predictable coefficients can undermine threshold security while passing every algebraic check. The public deterministic tweak does not protect a known or guessable imported secret.

Feldman commitments are not information-theoretically hiding: they expose public structure and permit checking coefficient guesses. Recovering a high-entropy scalar from its point relies on the hardness of the secp256k1 discrete logarithm problem. In particular, allowing a zero leading coefficient means the configured ''t'' is not a proof that a malicious dealer chose a polynomial of degree exactly ''t - 1''.

=== Share Protection and Commitment Agreement ===

Accepted shares are long-term signing secrets and MUST remain confidential. For honestly generated material, collecting ''t'' distinct shares permits reconstruction; loss of more than ''n - t'' shares prevents threshold signing without an external recovery mechanism. A ''1-of-n'' configuration gives every participant the complete secret.

A dealer can send individually valid shares under different commitments. The common-view requirement therefore matters independently of local verification and authenticated delivery. Shares from different commitments or ceremonies MUST NOT be combined.

=== Erasure and Implementation Security ===

Dealer erasure reduces the exposure period but cannot undo earlier disclosure. High-level runtimes, swap, crash dumps, storage, compilers, and hardware may retain residual copies; this does not remove the logical deletion requirement.

Operations on secret scalars SHOULD use mature implementations designed to resist timing and other relevant side channels, and SHOULD avoid secret-dependent control flow or memory access where practical. Pseudocode describes functional behavior and MUST NOT be treated as demonstrating constant-time execution, fault resistance, or secure memory handling.

=== Taproot and Signing Validation ===

The key-generation tweak is intended to address hidden script paths, subject to the review issue above. It does not make a malicious dealer safe: that dealer can already spend using the complete secret. Participants receive the final commitment, which alone does not prove how the dealer constructed the initial point or applied the tweak.

BIP 445 validation checks functional relationships needed for signing. It does not establish secure randomness, confidential delivery, agreement on commitments, or dealer erasure. Implementations MUST perform this specification's acceptance and distribution checks independently.

== Backward Compatibility ==

This proposal adds a key-generation procedure and changes no Bitcoin consensus rules, network protocol, wallet format, or BIP 445 signing algorithm. No consensus deployment mechanism is required.

Other key-generation conventions MUST NOT be assumed interoperable. In particular, identifier mapping, point encoding, the key-generation transformation, and the supported BIP 445 parameter range must agree. Importing an existing secret generally changes its public key and is not a procedure for preserving an existing output key.

== Test Vectors ==

The [[bip-frost-trusted-dealer-test-vectors.json|deterministic test vectors]] provide successful and failing cases. Scalars and points use the encodings specified in [[#Notation|Notation]] and [[#Parsing a VSS Commitment|Parsing a VSS Commitment]], represented as hexadecimal byte strings. Test coefficients are for verification, not production generation.

A successful vector SHOULD include ''n'', ''t'', initial coefficients and commitments, ''tau'', final coefficients and commitments, ''vss_commitment'', ''thresh_pk'', and each participant's identifier, share, and public share. It MUST permit checking the transformation, Feldman equation, and threshold-key derivation. Failure vectors SHOULD identify the failing operation and failure class; identical error messages are not required.

Coverage SHOULD include ''1-of-1'', a nontrivial threshold such as ''2-of-3'', ''t = n'', imported secrets, zero non-constant coefficients, and reconstruction from multiple signing subsets. Parameter-boundary tests SHOULD accept ''n = 128'' and reject ''n = 129'' in dealer generation, public-share derivation, share verification, and participant acceptance. Failure coverage SHOULD include invalid imported secrets and scalar encodings, malformed commitments, an infinity constant commitment, zero final shares, invalid identifiers, and tampered shares or commitments. Retry tests SHOULD confirm that zero final shares resample all non-constant coefficients while retaining the initial secret, and that Taproot-safety failures abort for both generated and imported secrets.

Implementations SHOULD cross-check the underlying Shamir and Feldman arithmetic against RFC 9591 where directly comparable, mapping RFC evaluation point ''x'' to ''id = x - 1''. Final outputs differ because this proposal additionally transforms the sharing.

== Reference Implementation ==

The [[python/trusted_dealer_ref/trusted_dealer_ref.py|Python reference implementation]] implements the deterministic construction and participant checks. Its [[python/trusted_dealer_ref/test_trusted_dealer_ref.py|algorithm tests]] and [[python/trusted_dealer_ref/test_test_vectors.py|vector tests]] exercise generation, verification, and reconstruction. The Python generation entry point takes ''(t, n, seckey)'', while the specification algorithm names its inputs ''(n, t, seckey)''.

The specification is normative. The Python implementation enforces ''1 ≤ t ≤ n ≤ 128'' for generation and participant checks. Its randomized wrapper retries zero final shares by resampling non-constant coefficients while retaining the initial secret; Taproot-safety failures abort the operation. The deterministic implementation and vectors use the additive transformation specified here.

The Python ''participant_key_accept'' function performs the local cryptographic checks and returns the participant identifier, secret share, threshold public key, own public share, and VSS commitment. The application retains ''n'' and ''t'', derives or retains the complete public-share list as needed, and establishes authenticated distribution and commitment agreement before declaring acceptance as required by [[#Participant Key Acceptance|Participant Key Acceptance]].

The reference implementation is for demonstration and interoperability testing. It is not constant-time and does not provide reliable secret-memory erasure. Passing its tests does not establish production security or resolve the Taproot-safety review issue.

